[36m(TaskRunner pid=330779)[0m [response] To determine where Alicia and Beth will meet, we can use the concept of relative speed. Since they are biking towards each other, their relative speed is the sum of their individual speeds. Alicia bikes at 18 miles per hour and Beth bikes at 12 miles per hour, so their relative speed is \(18 + 12 = 30\) miles per hour. This means that together, they cover 30 miles every hour.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=330779)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=330779)[0m Since the distance between the two cities is 45 miles, we can find the time it takes for them to meet by dividing the total distance by their relative speed:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=330779)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=330779)[0m \text{Time to meet} = \frac{45 \text{ miles}}{30 \text{ miles per hour}} = 1.5 \text{ hours}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=330779)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=330779)[0m Now, to find out how far Alicia has traveled in this time, we multiply her speed by the time:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=330779)[0m \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=330779)[0m \text{Distance Alicia travels} = 18 \text{ miles per hour} \times 1.5 \text{ hours} = 27 \text{ miles}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=330779)[0m \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=330779)[0m So, Alicia and Beth will meet 27 miles from City \(A\). The final answer is:
