[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m [response] To solve the equation \(2^8 = 4^x\), we can express both sides of the equation with the same base. Notice that \(4\) can be written as \(2^2\). Therefore, we can rewrite the equation as follows:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m \[2^8 = (2^2)^x\]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m Using the properties of exponents, \((a^m)^n = a^{mn}\), we can simplify the right side of the equation:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m \[2^8 = 2^{2x}\]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m Since the bases are the same, we can set the exponents equal to each other:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m \[8 = 2x\]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m 
