[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m [response] To solve the problem, we need to use the formula for the angular size of an object and the magnification formula for telescopes.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m 1. **Angular Diameter Calculation**:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m    The angular diameter \(\theta\) of the planet is given as \(45''\) (arcseconds). We need to convert this to radians because the formula for angular size uses radians. There are 3600 arcseconds in a degree and \(2\pi\) radians in a degree, so:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m    \theta = 45'' = \frac{45}{3600} \text{ degrees} = \frac{45}{3600} \times \frac{2\pi}{360} \text{ radians} = \frac{45 \times 2\pi}{3600 \times 360} = \frac{\pi}{1440} \text{ radians}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m 2. **Magnification Formula**:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m    The magnification \(M\) of a telescope is given by the ratio of the focal length of the telescope to the diameter of the objective lens (or mirror). For our telescope, the focal length \(f\) is 72 meters and the diameter \(D\) is 6.5 meters. So the magnification \(M\) is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m    M = \frac{f}{D} = \frac{72 \text{ m}}{6.5 \text{ m}} = \frac{72}{6.5} \approx 11.0769
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m 3. **Image Diameter Calculation**:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m    The angular size \(\theta\) of the planet is given by the formula:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m    \theta = \frac{\text{diameter of the image}}{\text{focal length}} \times \text{distance to the planet}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m    Since the angular size is small, we can use the small-angle approximation where \(\theta \approx \frac{\text{diameter of the image}}{\text{focal length}}\). Rearranging to solve for the diameter of the image, we get:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m    \text{diameter of the image} = \theta \times \text{focal length} = \frac{\pi}{1440} \times 72 \text{ m} = \frac{72\pi}{1440} \text{ m} = \frac{\pi}{20} \text{ m}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m    \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m    Converting this to centimeters:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m    \[
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m    \text{diameter of the image} = \frac{\pi}{20} \times 100 \text{ cm} = 5\pi \text{ cm} \approx 15.708 \text{ cm}
[36m(TaskRunner pid=383544)[0m    \]
